#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069709} :activity/id #uuid "c2e3d487-c981-55e6-ba25-8949d030cfa3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050274 :topic/id #uuid "3bdc5470-2e0b-5f3c-9875-854353ddeb19" :topic/math-academy-id 461} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069928 :tutorial/content "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2,$ as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ In other words, the limit of $f(x),$ as $x$ approaches $2,$ is $1$. We can express this mathematically as \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e52528a2-ed6a-5d07-89d4-1a74d70eb6b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 138 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "309b25fa-6c05-5421-8f80-e956e2975682" :step/math-academy-id 256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068428 :question/id #uuid "282add39-5184-589e-bb7b-b87a59cd41e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607443d5-b1dd-56b1-919b-fcc652ac3bc9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514441}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Function Value is Not Defined"} :step/id #uuid "01fbff48-2ebc-5e33-a1b9-e271896f5340" :step/math-academy-id 257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069717}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068533 :question/id #uuid "fb401fbe-a718-5ea7-b598-139ef837b047" :question/math-academy-id "e-2"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5fe1482-00d6-5738-b56a-8c082912d8d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514479}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit When the Limit is Not Equal to the Function Value"} :step/id #uuid "9144fd7b-81d4-5cb5-8051-0619e464002c" :step/math-academy-id 260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069924 :tutorial/content "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2,$ but they have the *same limit* at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$. \n\nNear $x=2,$ both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea300af0-adfe-5df2-8b4d-ae23430245a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1333 :tutorial/title "Limit Has Nothing to Do With the Function Value"} :step/id #uuid "d21275b2-0542-5145-8c14-535a8c0db329" :step/math-academy-id 2621 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068534 :question/id #uuid "ab95d805-958c-5de3-99bd-41368cc57080" :question/math-academy-id "e-3"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514469}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"} :step/id #uuid "0447b7b5-01f2-52cd-851f-84ee0d719836" :step/math-academy-id 263}] :activity/title "The Finite Limit of a Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068988}]}
